What is gall bladder cancer?
Gall bladder cancer is a relatively uncommon cancer that originates in the tissues of gall bladder and is often diagnosed late due to lack of signs and symptoms.
There is an important relationship between gallstones and gall bladder cancer, but there is no direct evidence for this.
It produces inflammation in the gall bladder.
These polyps may be formed either by cholesterol deposits in gall bladder, or may be caused by inflammation .
Polypoidal masses of the gall bladder are known to affect around 5% of adults .
Polyps larger than one centimetre are more likely to be cancerous.
If you have one or more risk factors, it does not imply that you will get gall bladder cancer certainly.
Jaundice is due to the accumulation of a bile salt called bilirubin (a chemical that gives bile its yellow colour) in the blood .
It can cause symptoms such as itching, pale urine or light colored stools.
It is seen as a fixed polypoidal mass projecting in to the lumen of the gallbladder with absence of acoustic shadowing or as an asymmetric thickening of the gallbladder wall.
Contrast Enhanced Computed Tomography scan (CECT)/Magnetic Resonance Imaging of the abdomen (MRI): The main advantage of CT lies in showing tumour infiltration into adjacent viscera or vessels,lymph node and distant metastases.
In a Jaundiced patient:
Tests and procedures used to diagnose gallbladder cancer include:
Blood tests.
This is called laparoscopy.
In this, the doctor makes a small cut on the stomach and a laparoscopy is inserted from it.
Your doctor may recommend surgery to look inside your abdomen for signs that gallbladder cancer has spread.
The camera allows the surgeon to examine organs surrounding your gallbladder for signs that the cancer has spread.
These tests can show blockages in the bile ducts.
Stage I: The cancer spreads to the inner lining of the gallbladder.
